<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stele of the Vultures</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2450 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/25th-century-bce">25th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Stele of the Vultures is a fragmentary limestone monument erected around 2450 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a>, to celebrate his victory over the neighbouring city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a>. It shows the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a> holding Lagash's enemies in a net and Eannatum leading a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> of soldiers, while vultures carry off the dead. Found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a> (Tello), it is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> and is one of the earliest known war monuments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures.jpg" alt="Stele of the Vultures" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Background: Kikuyu3 Elements: Eric Gaba (User:Sting) Composite: पाटलिपुत्र (talk) 10:52, 30 April 2020 (UTC) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_of_the_Vultures_in_the_Louvre_Museum_(enhanced_composite).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
